  "limits": [
    "Measured scheduling counts and target head size are joined to every original projection. For each request allow 1..min(6,scheduled) sampled suffix rows with the observed total M and every packed order. If every scheduled row is consumed, the v1 suffix formula gives an ordered prefix independently of request order. This is a source-and-runtime inference, not a dump of device indices. A nonzero-offset contiguous slice requires supported layout, alignment, address lifetime and validation.",
    "Raw-live slice removes only the two recorded device task times, keeping their outside gaps and projection. Any-head-pair-free removes them even for noncontiguous input as a free indexed-read sensitivity. Logit projections, complete vocabulary work, DSpark earlier checkpoints and expert computation stay fixed.",
    "With no external raw or dense-normalized reader, compute selected rows locally after the original HcPost, keep BF16 per-channel rounding, the original FP32 collapse tree, the BF16 collapse rounding and RMS. Each TP vocab shard must receive each selected row. A fixed-size gather sends K rows per original owner, padding to the largest sender. The original number of selected rows and the unchanged head projection dtype/shape are retained.",
    "Local costs use the median original 24-row five-task collapse plus RMS as a proxy for computing at most 19 selected rows per owner; arbitrary indexed loads, launch and synchronization can differ. Contiguous compact packets can feed the dense head as a slice. Fragmented packets pay the old selection-pair duration as a local reorder proxy. Per-rank BF16 gather times interpolate measured concurrent TP8 payload medians; a one-row transfer uses the three-row reference. Default and tuned calibrations are distinct scenarios. Raw removal must also be safe for capture and retirement.",
    "Original collective cost is latest exit minus latest entry within TP. Use existing clock-offset bounds in eager and clock-independent min/max local durations across graph ranks. Other native work is credited at group min/max and combined conservatively over DPs, with zero head credit for idle groups. This is a fixed-service timing sensitivity, not an observed speedup. P90 recomputed separately over all eight deadlines. Full-post-free grants the actual HcPost duration as well. This also bounds traffic-only or gather/head overlap at fixed head compute in the specified schedule; a changed launch gap or a different row demand needs its own evidence. Entire-post-to-head-free instead erases each live rank’s continuous elapsed time from final HcPost start to the first instruction of the original vocabulary GEMM, including waits and the graph-to-eager gap. This includes the full opportunity to overlap an unchanged head with its producer at fixed input readiness, resources and unchanged downstream work. Five-stage historical ceiling includes shared expert and merge work; retained for comparison only. Five-plus-graph also removes the measured graph readout; this is a deliberately wider scope.",
    "Ordinary uses its observed local prompt shape and graph starts with interpolated historical TP readout durations and median one-request decode cohorts, not same-request per-kernel timings. Single output row and no other reader assumed. Extra shared prompt work is not added."
  ],
